---

With the project, people, taxa, sites, and events in place, a specimen record
is mostly a matter of choosing from lists and typing measurements. The place,
coordinate, method, and possible collectors all come from the event you pick.

You enter the seven specimens chosen in
[Basic tutorial 1](/en/tutorials/basic/paper-catalog/). Each one shows a
different way paper records go wrong, and how to decide what to enter.

## From paper form to NAHPU

| Paper box | NAHPU field | Note |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `Collector No.` | Field ID | Heru's initials and number, shown as `HH436` |
| `Museum` | `Museum ID` | Under `Show more` |
| `Species` | `Taxon` | Chosen from the registry |
| `Locality`, `Trap or net line` | `Site ID`, then `Event ID` | In the capture record |
| `Capture date`, `Time` | `Capture date`, `Capture time` | Also enter the date as `Collection date` |
| `Collection method` | `Method` | Only the event's methods are offered |
| `Collector` | `Collector` | Only the event's people are offered |
| `TTL`, `TL`, `HF`, `Ear`, `WT (FA)` | Total, tail, hind foot, and ear length; weight | Head-and-body length is calculated |
| `male` / `female` and reproductive boxes | `Sex` and reproductive data | One sex; fields change with it |
| `Age` | `Life stage` | |
| `Preparation Code`, `Tissues in` | `Specimen Parts` | One part per piece of material |
| `Habitat` | Site habitat | Entered in Tutorial 3 |
| `Remarks` | `Remarks` | |

The preparation codes have no legend on the form. These tutorials read them as:

- `SA`: study skin, with the skull and skeleton cleaned and kept dry;
- `AL`: whole animal fixed in formalin, as a fluid specimen.

The `Tissues in` box records a tissue sample in ethanol. These tutorials enter
it as liver.

Make sure `Skin`, `Skull`, `Skeleton`, `Whole body`, and `Liver` are in
`Settings` > `Specimens` > `Specimen types`, and `None`, `ETOH`, and `Formalin`
in `Treatments`,
before you start.

## 1. Enter HH 436, the complete record

HH 436 is a squirrel caught on 12 September, the day the lines were set. The
journal for that day says, "I caught a squirrel right after setting the traps."
The two records agree.

{% steps %}

1. Open Specimens and select `+`. Check that the Field ID reads `HH436`. If it
   does not, use `Edit field ID` to correct the number.
2. In `Collection & Identification`:

    | Field | Value |
    | --- | --- |
    | `Cataloger` | `Heru Handika` (also fills `Preparator` and `Determiner`) |
    | `Museum ID` | `Z26230` |
    | `Collection date`, `Collection time` | 12 September 2014, `16:00` |
    | `Taxon` | *Lariscus insignis* |
    | `ID Confidence` | `High` |

    Leave `Prep. date` empty. The paper records only the capture.

3. In the capture record, choose `Site ID` `TJHH-L1`, then its event. Enter:

    | Field | Value |
    | --- | --- |
    | `Capture date`, `Capture time` | 12 September 2014, `16:00` |
    | `Method` | `Snap trap` |
    | `Coordinate ID` | `TJHH-PM` |
    | `Collector` | `Heru Handika` |

4. Enter the measurements: total length `238`, tail `84`, hind foot `46`,
   ear `18`, weight `120` g. Leave `Accuracy` as `Accurate`.
5. Set `Sex` to `Male`, testis `Position` to `Abdominal`, and `Life stage` to
   `Subadult`.
6. Under `Specimen Parts`, add four parts: `Skin`, `Skull`, and `Skeleton`,
   each with treatment `None`; and `Liver` with treatment `ETOH`. The paper
   code `SA` becomes three separate parts, so each can be stored, loaned, and
   tracked on its own.

{% /steps %}

Open the record again. Without typing a locality, it shows Indonesia, Jambi,
Kerinci, Pondok Merah, the coordinate, and the habitat, all from the site.

![NAHPU Specimen Records page on an iPad for HH436, Lariscus insignis, with museum ID Z26230, collection on 12 September 2014 at 4:00 PM, site TJHH-L1, method Snap trap, and coordinate TJHH-PM](../../../../../assets/images/app-screenshots/tutorials/basic/specimen-hh436.webp)

Further down the same record are the measurements and parts. NAHPU calculates
head-and-body length (154 mm) and the tail ratio from the values you typed:

![NAHPU Specimen Attributes and Specimen Parts for HH436 on an iPad: total length 238, tail 84, hind foot 46, ear 18, weight 120 g, male, subadult; parts Skin, Skull, and Skeleton with no treatment, and Liver in ETOH](../../../../../assets/images/app-screenshots/tutorials/basic/specimen-hh436-attributes.webp)

## 2. Enter the other six

Use the same steps with the values below. The notes after the table explain the
choices.

| Field ID | `Museum ID` | `Taxon` | Site | Capture date and time | `Method` | `Collector` | TTL–TL–HF–Ear, weight | `Sex` | `Life stage` | Parts |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `HH437` | `Z26231` | *Mus crociduroides* | `TJHH-L1` | 13 Sep 2014, `07:00` | `Snap trap` | Heru Handika | 191–103–22–16, 16 g | `Female` | `Subadult` | Skin; skull; skeleton; liver, ETOH |
| `HH442` | `Z26236` | *Maxomys hylomyoides* | `TJHH-L1` | 13 Sep 2014, empty | `Snap trap` | Heru Handika | 238–119–30–18, 46 g | `Female` | Empty | Whole body, Formalin |
| `HH453` | `Z26247` | *Crocidura beccarii* | `TJHH-P1` | 13 Sep 2014, empty | `Pitfall` | Heru Handika | 150–75–17–8, 7 g | `Male` | `Adult` | Whole body, Formalin; liver, ETOH |
| `HH456` | `Z26250` | *Maxomys hylomyoides* | `TJBS-L2` | 13 Sep 2014, `07:00` | `Snap trap` | Heru Handika | 221–113–28–16, 48 g | `Male`, testes abdominal | `Subadult` | Whole body, Formalin; liver, ETOH |
| `HH523` | `Z26317` | *Maxomys* (genus) | `TJHH-L3` | 17 Sep 2014, relative time `Morning` | `Snap trap` | Bima Sampel | 268–124–29–18, 66 g | `Male`, testes 17 × 4 mm | `Adult` | Skin; skull; skeleton; liver, ETOH |
| `HH529` | Empty | *Crocidura neglecta* | `TJHH-P4` | 17 Sep 2014, relative time `Morning` | `Pitfall` | Bima Sampel | 112–45–11–7, 4.4 g | `Male` | `Adult` | Whole body, Formalin; liver, ETOH |

Copy the dates to `Collection date` as well. For the Pasir Putih specimens,
leave `Coordinate ID` empty: those sites have no coordinate.

### HH 437: overwritten number and sex

On paper, `437` is written over `436`, and `male` is scribbled out with
`female` circled. Both corrections look deliberate, so enter `HH437` and
`Female`. In NAHPU these mistakes are harder to make: the Field ID comes from
Heru's running number, not from memory, and `Sex` holds one value. Changing it
asks `Change sex?` and clears the reproductive fields that no longer apply,
rather than leaving both sets on the form.

Add to `Remarks`:
`Paper form: field number written over 436; sex corrected from male to female.`

### HH 442: the date that breaks the sequence

The form says `17 Sept`, but the evidence points to 13 September:

- HH 441 and HH 443, on either side of it, were both caught on 13 September;
- its museum number, Z26236, falls between theirs, Z26235 and Z26237;
- the 17 September captures start much later, at HH 512.

Enter 13 September. The form also leaves the trap line, time, and age blank.
Leave the time and life stage empty. A specimen needs an event, so link it to
`TJHH-L1`, where HH 438–441 were caught, and say that this is an inference.
The `Tissues in` box is not circled, so add no liver part.

Add to `Remarks`:
`Paper form gives capture date 17 Sep 2014; entered as 13 Sep 2014 from the record sequence and museum numbers. Trap line not recorded; assigned to Line 1 as for HH 438–441.`

### HH 453: a pitfall capture on "Line 1"

The form says line `1` and method `Pitfall`. On paper, Line 1 was both
pitfalls and snap traps, so the line number alone cannot tell you which. In
NAHPU you choose the `TJHH-P1` event, and `Method` offers only `Pitfall`. The
neighboring record, HH 452, shows why this matters: its method was first
written as `Snap Trap`, then crossed out and replaced with `Pitfall`.

### HH 456: a changed identification

On paper, `Mus crociduroides` is crossed out and `Maxomys hylomyoides` written
after it, with no name or date for the change. Enter the current name and set
`ID Confidence` to `Medium`, since the identification changed in the field.
`Determiner` already names who made it.

Add to `Remarks`:
`First recorded in the field as Mus crociduroides.`

When a specialist re-identifies the specimen later, they update `Taxon`,
`Determiner`, and `ID Confidence` in the same record.

### HH 523: the possible new species

Heru wrote `Maxomys cf. hylomyoides`: it looks like *M. hylomyoides*, but he was
not sure. Choose the genus *Maxomys* from the registry and set `ID Confidence`
to `High`, because the genus is certain.

The form's time, `09:00`, does not fit the journal. On 17 September the team
left camp at 08:00, and the boat ride to Pasir Putih took about 1.5 hours.
Heru was in camp that morning, so the collector was someone from the boat team.
Choose `Bima Sampel`, who led that team, and use `Relative time` `Morning`
instead of an exact time no one recorded.

Add to `Remarks`:
`cf. hylomyoides. Underparts darker than M. hylomyoides: more orange at the base and grey on the belly. Paper time 09:00 conflicts with the trap check; recorded as morning.`

### HH 529: an impossible time and a misused box

The form says `07.00`, an hour before the team left camp. Many forms in this
catalog read `07.00`, which looks like a habit rather than a measurement. Use
`Relative time` `Morning` and `Bima Sampel` as collector, as for HH 523.

The `Museum` box says `Blank Tube`, which is not a museum number. Leave
`Museum ID` empty and add the note to the liver part's `Remarks`:
`Tube label left blank in 2014.` Museum and tissue numbers have separate fields
in NAHPU, so this kind of note no longer needs a box of its own.

Add to the specimen's `Remarks`:
`Paper time 07:00 conflicts with the trap check (team left camp at 08:00); recorded as morning.`

In the finished record, `Relative time` is on and the capture time reads
`Morning`. The site and event say Pasir Putih, and `Coordinate ID` is empty
because that site has no coordinate:

![NAHPU Specimen Records page on an iPad for HH529, Crocidura neglecta, captured on 17 September 2014 in the morning at TJHH-P4 by pitfall, with no coordinate](../../../../../assets/images/app-screenshots/tutorials/basic/specimen-hh529.webp)

{% aside type="tip" title="Faster entry for similar specimens" %}
Many specimens share the same parts. Open a finished record and choose
`Duplicate part` from the three-dot menu. NAHPU creates a new specimen with the
same part types and treatments. Tissue IDs, dates, and remarks are not copied.
{% /aside %}

## 3. Check the records

{% steps %}

1. Turn on `Show outlier warnings` and review any flagged measurements.
2. Open each record and check the Field ID against the paper form.
3. Check that the three Pasir Putih specimens show Pasir Putih, not Pondok
   Merah.
4. Check that HH 453 and HH 529 show `Pitfall`, and the others `Snap trap`.

{% /steps %}

## What you gained over paper

- No specimen carries a locality of its own, so none can carry the wrong one.
- Method and collector come from the event, so a pitfall capture cannot be
  filed as a snap-trap capture, and the collector is someone who was there.
- Each name, sex, and life stage is chosen from a list, with one value.
- Every correction is explained in `Remarks` instead of being written over.
- Parts are explicit records, not codes that need a legend.

